Package: wwan-manager
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: all
Maintainer: @MAINTAINER@
Section: net
Priority: optional
Depends: ifupdown, iproute2, util-linux, coreutils, python3, systemd, udev, debconf (>= 0.5) | debconf-2.0
Description: on-demand LTE data connection for a Sierra Wireless EM7455 / Dell DW5811e
 Brings up a Sierra Wireless EM7455 (1199:9071) or Dell DW5811e (413c:81b6,
 or converted to 1199:9071) in QMI configuration as an ifupdown interface
 (default wwan0), dialed on demand: "ifup wwan0" starts a QMI data session
 (/usr/lib/wwan-manager/qmi) and sets address, gateway, MTU and DNS from the
 modem (raw IP), "ifdown wwan0" hangs up. Nothing dials by itself. The LTE
 routes go into their own routing table (default 700), so normal traffic
 stays on the primary uplink. Sends the per-session FCC unlock before every
 dial. Dial progress and failure diagnostics go to the journal (tag
 wwan-dial). /etc/resolv.conf is never touched; the LTE nameservers (DNS
 setting, default 1.1.1.1, or the modem's) are recorded in
 /run/wwan/<iface>.dns.
 .
 wwan-sim shows or switches the active SIM on dual-SIM carrier boards,
 wwan-pin shows the SIM PIN state or switches the SIM's PIN lock off,
 wwan-apn shows or sets the APN (stored in the modem's PDP context).
 Settings are in /etc/default/wwan-manager.
